# Curly Brackets in Condition Blocks and Arrow Functions

Always use curly brackets `{}` in condition blocks, loop blocks, and arrow function bodies, even when the block contains only one statement. This applies to `if`, `else`, `for`, `while`, `do...while`, and arrow functions (`=>`).

## Condition and loop blocks

```javascript
// ❌ BAD – single statement without braces
if (num > 5) return;
if (num > 5)
  return;
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) doSomething(i);

// ✅ GOOD – braces always used
if (num > 5) {
  return;
}
if (num > 5) {
  return;
}
for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
  doSomething(i);
}
```

```javascript
// ❌ BAD – else without braces
if (ok) doA();
else doB();

// ✅ GOOD
if (ok) {
  doA();
} else {
  doB();
}
```

## Arrow functions

```javascript
// ❌ BAD – implicit return, no braces
const startNewGame = (wordLength: WordLength) =>
  mutation.mutateAsync(wordLength);

const double = (x) => x * 2;

// ✅ GOOD – braces and explicit return
const startNewGame = (wordLength: WordLength) => {
  return mutation.mutateAsync(wordLength);
};

const double = (x) => {
  return x * 2;
};
```

## Why

- Reduces risk of bugs when adding a second statement later.
- Keeps style consistent and easier to read.
- Avoids ambiguity with nested conditions.
- For arrow functions: makes return behavior explicit and keeps bodies consistent.
